{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A8wSYbjXy6jx1msdwjAV3Q","type":"tool_result","content":"22:    In an assembly of phantasms such as I have painted, it may well be supposed that no ordinary appearance could have excited such sensation. In truth the masquerade license of the night was nearly unlimited; but the figure in question had out-Heroded Herod, and gone beyond the bounds of even the prince's indefinite decorum. There are chords in the hearts of the most reckless which cannot be touched without emotion. Even with the utterly lost, to whom life and death are equally jests, there are matters of which no jest can be made. The whole company, indeed, seemed now deeply to feel that in the costume and bearing of the stranger neither wit nor propriety existed. The figure was tall and gaunt, and shrouded from head to foot in the habiliments of the grave. The mask which concealed the visage was made so nearly to resemble the countenance of a stiffened corpse that the closest scrutiny must have had difficulty in detecting the cheat. And yet all this might have been endured, if not approved, by the mad revellers around. But the mummer had gone so far as to assume the type of the Red Death. His vesture was dabbled in blood -- and his broad brow, with all the features of the face, was besprinkled with the scarlet horror. \n30:    It was in the blue room where stood the prince, with a group of pale courtiers by his side. At first, as he spoke, there was a slight rushing movement of this group in the direction of the intruder, who, at the moment was also near at hand, and now, with deliberate and stately step, made closer approach to the speaker. But from a certain nameless awe with which the mad assumptions of the mummer had inspired the whole party, there were found none who put forth hand to seize him; so that, unimpeded, he passed within a yard of the prince's person; and, while the vast assembly, as if with one impulse, shrank from the centres of the rooms to the walls, he made his way uninterruptedly, but with the same solemn and measured step which had distinguished him from the first, through the blue chamber to the purple -- through the purple to the green -- through the green to the orange -- through this again to the white -- and even thence to the violet, ere a decided movement had been made to arrest him. It was then, however, that the Prince Prospero, maddening with rage and the shame of his own momentary cowardice, rushed hurriedly through the six chambers, while none followed him on account of a deadly terror that had seized upon all. He bore aloft a drawn dagger, and had approached, in rapid impetuosity, to within three or four feet of the retreating figure, when the latter, having attained the extremity of the velvet apartment, turned suddenly and confronted his pursuer. There was a sharp cry -- and the dagger dropped gleaming upon the sable carpet, upon which, instantly afterwards, fell prostrate in death the Prince Prospero. Then, summoning the wild courage of despair, a throng of the revellers at once threw themselves into the black apartment, and, seizing the mummer, whose tall figure stood erect and motionless within the shadow of the ebony clock, gasped in unutterable horror at finding the grave cerements and corpse-like mask which they handled with so violent a rudeness, untenanted by any tangible form.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06d97e30-a327-45f1-a839-98445bc7c8b1","uuid":"824823fa-29a6-49cd-8be3-66b67a74c138","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"22:    In an assembly of phantasms such as I have painted, it may well be supposed that no ordinary appearance could have excited such sensation. In truth the masquerade license of the night was nearly unlimited; but the figure in question had out-Heroded Herod, and gone beyond the bounds of even the prince's indefinite decorum. There are chords in the hearts of the most reckless which cannot be touched without emotion. Even with the utterly lost, to whom life and death are equally jests, there are matters of which no jest can be made. The whole company, indeed, seemed now deeply to feel that in the costume and bearing of the stranger neither wit nor propriety existed. The figure was tall and gaunt, and shrouded from head to foot in the habiliments of the grave. The mask which concealed the visage was made so nearly to resemble the countenance of a stiffened corpse that the closest scrutiny must have had difficulty in detecting the cheat. And yet all this might have been endured, if not approved, by the mad revellers around. But the mummer had gone so far as to assume the type of the Red Death. His vesture was dabbled in blood -- and his broad brow, with all the features of the face, was besprinkled with the scarlet horror. \n30:    It was in the blue room where stood the prince, with a group of pale courtiers by his side. At first, as he spoke, there was a slight rushing movement of this group in the direction of the intruder, who, at the moment was also near at hand, and now, with deliberate and stately step, made closer approach to the speaker. But from a certain nameless awe with which the mad assumptions of the mummer had inspired the whole party, there were found none who put forth hand to seize him; so that, unimpeded, he passed within a yard of the prince's person; and, while the vast assembly, as if with one impulse, shrank from the centres of the rooms to the walls, he made his way uninterruptedly, but with the same solemn and measured step which had distinguished him from the first, through the blue chamber to the purple -- through the purple to the green -- through the green to the orange -- through this again to the white -- and even thence to the violet, ere a decided movement had been made to arrest him. It was then, however, that the Prince Prospero, maddening with rage and the shame of his own momentary cowardice, rushed hurriedly through the six chambers, while none followed him on account of a deadly terror that had seized upon all. He bore aloft a drawn dagger, and had approached, in rapid impetuosity, to within three or four feet of the retreating figure, when the latter, having attained the extremity of the velvet apartment, turned suddenly and confronted his pursuer. There was a sharp cry -- and the dagger dropped gleaming upon the sable carpet, upon which, instantly afterwards, fell prostrate in death the Prince Prospero. Then, summoning the wild courage of despair, a throng of the revellers at once threw themselves into the black apartment, and, seizing the mummer, whose tall figure stood erect and motionless within the shadow of the ebony clock, gasped in unutterable horror at finding the grave cerements and corpse-like mask which they handled with so violent a rudeness, untenanted by any tangible form.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_01NKEMBvLYXajgdvJ2CKwpos","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I can see the issue clearly. From the text:\n\n1. Line 22: \"But **the mummer** had gone so far as to assume the type of the Red Death\" - This is describing the MASKED FIGURE/INTRUDER, NOT Prince Prospero\n2. Line 30: \"the mad assumptions of **the mummer** had inspired the whole party\" - Again, the intruder\n3. Line 30: \"seizing **the mummer**, whose tall figure stood erect and motionless\" - The revellers seize the mummer AFTER Prince Prospero has died\n\nSo \"the mummer\" is clearly the Red Death figure, the antagonist, NOT Prince Prospero.\n\nNow let me trace where this happens in the consensus pipeline. The issue is in the `_llm_cross_group_resolution()` which links epithets to proper names. Let me check what context is being provided to the LLM:\n\n## Root Cause Documentation\n\n### Issue: \"the mummer\" merged with \"Prince Prospero\"\n- **Symptom:** \"the mummer\" appears as an alias of \"the Prince Prospero\" in character list\n- **Data flow trace:**\n  1. Appears in: output/masque_of_red_death/analysis.json and report.html\n  2. Stored in: AnalysisResult.characters[0].aliases\n  3. Generated by: CharacterAgent.run()\n  4. **Originates in:** src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py:_llm_cross_group_resolution():line 1949-2024\n- **Root cause:** The LLM is asked to link epithets to proper names based on limited context (3 snippets of 100 chars each). In \"Masque of the Red Death\", both \"Prince Prospero\" and \"the mummer\" appear in the same scene, and without sufficient context, the LLM incorrectly infers they refer to the same character. The actual text shows:\n  - \"the mummer\" is the RED DEATH figure (the antagonist/intruder)\n  - Prince Prospero CONFRONTS \"the mummer\" and is killed by it\n  - They are clearly DIFFERENT characters in conflict\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n\nThe problem is that the cross-group resolution logic provides:\n- Only 3 context snippets of max 100 chars each (line 1973)\n- Only chapter co-occurrence info\n- But NOT enough narrative context to distinguish that two characters appear in the SAME SCENE but are DIFFERENT PEOPLE\n\nLet me check a failed approach from ATTEMPT_1_SUMMARY.md to ensure I don't retry something that failed:\n\nLooking at ATTEMPT_1_SUMMARY.md, the failed approaches were all related to character MERGING (preventing family member merges, filtering ambiguous names). This is a different problem - it's about PREVENTING an incorrect merge by providing better context or adding conflict detection.\n\nNow let me implement a fix. The key insight is: if an epithet appears in contexts where it's clearly in OPPOSITION to or DISTINCT from the proper name, they should NOT be merged."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":875,"cache_read_input_tokens":46994,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":875,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06d97e30-a327-45f1-a839-98445bc7c8b1","uuid":"4d9990bc-50e4-4db1-8791-7fe58224ef53"}
