{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SKm6GAVRb5KjxNHbnu9rMj","type":"tool_result","content":"    96→CROSS_GROUP_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether descriptive handles (epithets) refer to named characters.\n    97→\n    98→Examples:\n    99→- \"the prince\" might refer to \"Prince Prospero\"\n   100→- \"the duke\" might refer to \"Prince Prospero\" (if the story mentions he's also a duke)\n   101→- \"the creature\" might refer to \"Adam\" (if named)\n   102→- \"my father\" might refer to \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"\n   103→\n   104→CRITICAL RULES:\n   105→1. Only link when context CLEARLY indicates they're the same person\n   106→2. Use chapter co-appearances and context clues\n   107→3. Titles/roles (the king, the general, my father) can refer to properly-named characters\n   108→4. Generic epithets that could be anyone should NOT be linked\n   109→\n   110→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n   111→\n   112→CROSS_GROUP_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to properly-named characters.\n   113→\n   114→DESCRIPTIVE HANDLES:\n   115→{epithets}\n   116→\n   117→NAMED CHARACTERS:\n   118→{proper_names}\n   119→\n   120→For each epithet that CLEARLY refers to a named character, return a match.\n   121→\n   122→Return JSON array:\n   123→```json\n   124→[\n   125→  {{\"epithet\": \"the prince\", \"proper_name\": \"Prince Prospero\"}},\n   126→  {{\"epithet\": \"my father\", \"proper_name\": \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"}}\n   127→]\n   128→```\n   129→\n   130→Return ONLY the JSON array. Include only matches you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06d97e30-a327-45f1-a839-98445bc7c8b1","uuid":"65fc00f3-fc4b-4297-b8b7-983cadf037d1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py","content":"CROSS_GROUP_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether descriptive handles (epithets) refer to named characters.\n\nExamples:\n- \"the prince\" might refer to \"Prince Prospero\"\n- \"the duke\" might refer to \"Prince Prospero\" (if the story mentions he's also a duke)\n- \"the creature\" might refer to \"Adam\" (if named)\n- \"my father\" might refer to \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"\n\nCRITICAL RULES:\n1. Only link when context CLEARLY indicates they're the same person\n2. Use chapter co-appearances and context clues\n3. Titles/roles (the king, the general, my father) can refer to properly-named characters\n4. Generic epithets that could be anyone should NOT be linked\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n\nCROSS_GROUP_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to properly-named characters.\n\nDESCRIPTIVE HANDLES:\n{epithets}\n\nNAMED CHARACTERS:\n{proper_names}\n\nFor each epithet that CLEARLY refers to a named character, return a match.\n\nReturn JSON array:\n```json\n[\n  {{\"epithet\": \"the prince\", \"proper_name\": \"Prince Prospero\"}},\n  {{\"epithet\": \"my father\", \"proper_name\": \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"}}\n]\n```\n\nReturn ONLY the JSON array. Include only matches you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"","numLines":35,"startLine":96,"totalLines":2066}}}
